1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,186
The Run on the Banks, 1914:

2
00:00:02,266 --> 00:00:05,875
When War in Europe
Emptied a Singapore Bank

3
00:00:05,875 --> 00:00:10,224
On Tuesday the 4th of August
1914, the day before news of

4
00:00:10,304 --> 00:00:13,409
war in Europe was published in Singapore,

5
00:00:13,489 --> 00:00:18,850
depositors began to rush the Chinese
Commercial Bank on Kling Street.

6
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:23,515
By the next evening they had
taken out more than $300,000,

7
00:00:23,595 --> 00:00:26,050
and the bank closed its doors.

8
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:29,415
It would stay shut for
eight weeks, and the story

9
00:00:29,495 --> 00:00:33,071
of how it reopened says a
good deal about how a bank

10
00:00:33,151 --> 00:00:36,525
could be saved before
deposit insurance existed.

11
00:00:36,525 --> 00:00:41,800
The Chinese Commercial Bank was the
first Hokkien bank in Singapore.

12
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,927
It had opened in December 1912,

13
00:00:45,007 --> 00:00:50,075
and by 1914 it had $1
million in paid-up capital.

14
00:00:50,075 --> 00:00:55,158
It also opened only a year before
Singapore's first Chinese bank,

15
00:00:55,238 --> 00:00:59,606
the Kwong Yik Bank, failed
after a run in November 1913,

16
00:00:59,686 --> 00:01:05,325
a collapse described in our post on
the Chinese banks that became OCBC.

17
00:01:05,325 --> 00:01:09,530
The Straits Times later wrote that
the Kwong Yik affair had shaken

18
00:01:09,610 --> 00:01:13,880
confidence in Chinese banking more
than anything else, so that when

19
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:18,050
the war alarm came, the Chinese
rushed to draw out their money.

20
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:22,035
According to the same editorial,
it was "generally understood"

21
00:01:22,115 --> 00:01:25,183
that a day or two before
the declaration of war,

22
00:01:25,263 --> 00:01:29,707
"certain communications were made
to the leading European banks here,

23
00:01:29,787 --> 00:01:33,000
so that they could prepare
for a possible panic."

24
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,090
The Chinese Commercial
Bank, the paper added,

25
00:01:36,170 --> 00:01:38,425
"did not share in that warning."

26
00:01:38,425 --> 00:01:42,300
The editorial set out
what happened, day by day.

27
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:49,265
On the 4th of August, the day before
the news of war was published,

28
00:01:49,345 --> 00:01:53,025
$257,667 was paid over the counter.

29
00:01:53,025 --> 00:01:58,738
The next day the run continued,
and despite delays in paying,

30
00:01:58,818 --> 00:02:03,012
another $50,000 was
drawn out, so that by the

31
00:02:03,092 --> 00:02:08,125
close of business the bank
had only $167,268 in cash.

32
00:02:08,125 --> 00:02:11,687
Its secretary, Seow Poh Leng,
wrote to the Malaya Tribune

33
00:02:11,767 --> 00:02:16,161
that the directors "have had to
request the assistance of Government",

34
00:02:16,241 --> 00:02:18,461
and that the government had promised

35
00:02:18,541 --> 00:02:21,800
help pending an examination
of the bank's accounts.

36
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,550
The bank was closed while
the inspection went on.

37
00:02:25,550 --> 00:02:28,870
On the 26th of August
the government posted a

38
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:31,968
proclamation in Chinese outside the bank,

39
00:02:32,048 --> 00:02:35,750
signed by the acting
Colonial Secretary, Maxwell.

40
00:02:35,750 --> 00:02:39,864
It said the government had "examined
thoroughly the accounts of the

41
00:02:39,944 --> 00:02:44,496
Chinese Commercial Bank and finds that
its assets exceed the liabilities",

42
00:02:44,576 --> 00:02:48,126
and that the trouble was "partly
due to the fact that many

43
00:02:48,206 --> 00:02:52,525
traders are hoarding their money
instead of paying it into the bank."

44
00:02:52,525 --> 00:02:55,005
If the shareholders could raise money,

45
00:02:55,085 --> 00:02:58,509
either in cash or by a call
on their unpaid capital,

46
00:02:58,589 --> 00:03:02,025
the government would lend
the bank an equal amount.

47
00:03:02,025 --> 00:03:05,023
It ended with a warning
to everyone in the colony:

48
00:03:05,103 --> 00:03:08,654
people who had cash should pay
it into the banks so that it

49
00:03:08,734 --> 00:03:12,101
could circulate and help
commerce, because hoarding "not

50
00:03:12,181 --> 00:03:15,548
only does no good to those
who hoard, but will certainly

51
00:03:15,628 --> 00:03:19,075
do harm to the local situation
and thus help the enemy."

52
00:03:19,075 --> 00:03:22,295
The Malaya Tribune headlined
it "Stern Warning to

53
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:25,675
Hoarders of Money, Whose
Action Helps the Enemy."

54
00:03:25,675 --> 00:03:28,659
The Teochew community's
bank, Sze Hai Tong,

55
00:03:28,739 --> 00:03:31,875
came through the same
panic without closing.

56
00:03:31,875 --> 00:03:36,090
According to the National Library
Board's account, it met its

57
00:03:36,170 --> 00:03:39,540
customers' withdrawals
from strong cash reserves,

58
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:44,609
the Teochew business community stood
by it, and its largest shareholder,

59
00:03:44,689 --> 00:03:49,125
Low Peng Yam, put $500,000 into
the bank to restore confidence.

60
00:03:49,125 --> 00:03:52,932
On the 24th of September
the Straits Times reported

61
00:03:53,012 --> 00:03:56,667
that the bank was expected
to reopen within days,

62
00:03:56,747 --> 00:04:00,859
once an agreement was signed
under which the government

63
00:04:00,939 --> 00:04:04,750
would lend it up to $350,000
on adequate security.

64
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:09,750
It reopened at ten o'clock on
Thursday the 1st of October.

65
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:13,003
According to the Straits
Echo, Kling Street that

66
00:04:13,083 --> 00:04:17,170
morning "was as muddy as a
goal-mouth after a day's deluge",

67
00:04:17,250 --> 00:04:20,375
but from traffic rather
than from depositors.

68
00:04:20,375 --> 00:04:24,705
A couple of Malay policemen
and a watchman stood ready at

69
00:04:24,785 --> 00:04:29,424
the doors "to cope with any
unseemly rushes", but within five

70
00:04:29,504 --> 00:04:33,369
minutes more money had been
paid in than taken out,

71
00:04:33,449 --> 00:04:38,400
and by noon the bank had received
$27,552 more than it paid out.

72
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:41,782
Afterwards the bank kept
a much larger share of

73
00:04:41,862 --> 00:04:45,325
its money in cash, choosing
safety over profit.

74
00:04:45,325 --> 00:04:50,945
In 1914, a bank in trouble depended
on its community standing behind it,

75
00:04:51,025 --> 00:04:54,350
and on the government deciding to step in.

76
00:04:54,350 --> 00:04:58,250
Today the safety net
is set out in advance.

77
00:04:58,250 --> 00:05:02,776
Singapore has had a deposit
insurance scheme since 2006,

78
00:05:02,856 --> 00:05:07,050
run by the Singapore Deposit
Insurance Corporation.

79
00:05:07,050 --> 00:05:12,295
Since April 2024 it covers
Singapore dollar deposits of up to

80
00:05:12,375 --> 00:05:16,747
$100,000 per depositor at
each member bank, enough,

81
00:05:16,827 --> 00:05:23,200
according to the corporation, to cover
91 per cent of depositors in full.

82
00:05:23,200 --> 00:05:27,486
In October 2008, during the
global financial crisis,

83
00:05:27,566 --> 00:05:32,525
the government went further and
guaranteed all bank deposits

84
00:05:32,605 --> 00:05:37,475
until the end of 2010, backed
by $150 billion of reserves.

85
00:05:37,475 --> 00:05:41,025
No claims were made under the guarantee.

86
00:05:41,025 --> 00:05:43,050
Could it happen again?

87
00:05:43,050 --> 00:05:46,126
Several of the conditions behind the

88
00:05:46,206 --> 00:05:49,450
1914 run are now dealt with directly.

89
00:05:49,450 --> 00:05:54,328
Banks in Singapore are supervised by
the Monetary Authority of Singapore,

90
00:05:54,408 --> 00:05:57,181
which requires them to hold minimum levels

91
00:05:57,261 --> 00:06:00,577
of capital and of liquid
assets that can be turned

92
00:06:00,657 --> 00:06:04,516
into cash quickly, so that a
sudden wave of withdrawals is

93
00:06:04,596 --> 00:06:08,400
less likely to empty the till
as it did on Kling Street.

94
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:11,580
Most ordinary depositors
are fully insured,

95
00:06:11,660 --> 00:06:15,375
which removes much of the
reason to queue at all.

96
00:06:15,375 --> 00:06:20,125
But runs have not disappeared,
and they have become much faster.

97
00:06:20,125 --> 00:06:24,297
On the 9th of March 2023,
customers of Silicon Valley Bank

98
00:06:24,377 --> 00:06:28,550
in the United States withdrew
about 42 billion US dollars,

99
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:32,215
almost a quarter of its
deposits, in a single day,

100
00:06:32,295 --> 00:06:36,468
much of it above the insured
limit, and requests for about

101
00:06:36,548 --> 00:06:40,800
100 billion US dollars more
were waiting the next morning.

102
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:44,600
Regulators closed the
bank on the 10th of March.

103
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:47,946
In 1914 the run on the
Chinese Commercial Bank

104
00:06:48,026 --> 00:06:51,745
took two days; phone banking
and messages spreading

105
00:06:51,825 --> 00:06:55,400
online meant this one
took little more than one.

106
00:06:55,400 --> 00:06:59,516
The lesson that carries over from
Kling Street is that confidence

107
00:06:59,596 --> 00:07:03,003
is what holds a bank together,
and that the safeguards

108
00:07:03,083 --> 00:07:07,150
built since then are meant to
protect it before a panic starts.

109
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:11,530
Where it fits in the bigger
story: The Chinese Commercial Bank

110
00:07:11,610 --> 00:07:16,998
survived the panic of August 1914 because
the government examined its books,

111
00:07:17,078 --> 00:07:20,675
said publicly that it was
sound and lent it money.

112
00:07:20,675 --> 00:07:25,141
A century later, deposit insurance,
the 2008 guarantee and the

113
00:07:25,221 --> 00:07:31,153
rules on capital and liquidity were built
to make that kind of rescue unnecessary,

114
00:07:31,233 --> 00:07:34,599
by giving ordinary
depositors no reason to run,

115
00:07:34,679 --> 00:07:39,225
though a run can now move far
faster than one on Kling Street.
